A||||5||84 2in1||Duke buildup opinions (Sorry LONG)||||6||
Z||000000||84 2in1||05-20-2001||09:42 PM||||For a foundation use the Astro van block(solid instead of windowed mains). A 153 C.I. marine crank (think this is a Chevy part?????).Stuff in Chevy small journal rods after the beams are polished out. May have to use custom pistons to get the correct wrist pin deck height???For a top end machine out the head for 194I,1.60E Valves.port out a little to reshape the Intake side(remove the little cast in turbulence bump). then send in the Intake manifold and head and have them extrude honed for portmaching and airflow then match the exhaust to a header.Then use a holley TBI?Any other ideas?-B-||||reg||6
Z||000001||Cooter||05-20-2001||10:03 PM||||An old boat we had was powered by a GM 4 cylinder Duke like engine. It was something like 180 C.I. and 150 h/p. We would run it at 5000 rpms for long distances and never had any trouble from it. Why not use that whole engine, less the boat exhaust manifold of course.||||reg||1
Z||000002||88formula||05-20-2001||10:22 PM||||Why would you want to remove the cast in part in the combustion chambers that swirls the air and gas? The rest sounds good but expensive for the gains you would get.  <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||05-21-2001||10:07 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cooter:<BR><B>An old boat we had was powered by a GM 4 cylinder Duke like engine. It was something like 180 C.I. and 150 h/p. We would run it at 5000 rpms for long distances and never had any trouble from it. Why not use that whole engine, less the boat exhaust manifold of course.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I believe the marine engine is still a Chevy block. Seems like it had the Chevy trans bolt pattern. We had a MerCruiser 120 with the same block. 153 C.I. 120 HP. This was early 70s.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)||||reg||1
Z||000004||erikred||05-21-2001||03:05 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 84 2in1:<BR><B>For a foundation use the Astro van block(solid instead of windowed mains).  then send in the Intake manifold and head and have them extrude honed for portmaching and airflow then match the exhaust to a header. Any other ideas?-B-</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Isn't the Astro block RWD? If it is you would be better off buying a Merc. 181 ,using an adapter plate and swapping the heads.<P>I would not bother with extrude honing. I think the benifits compared to a good regular port job (wich you can do yourself and save $$) are minimal. For what it cost you could almost buy a new alum race head and probably have better flow and less weight. <P>Use metal timming gears and a turbo.||||reg||14
Z||000005||85Josh||05-21-2001||09:16 PM||||I love this! Already up to four posts and no one has said "put in a v-6!". I belive that mrossum here on the forum is using the Chevy 153 crank, it is supposedly forged, not cast. I do know that the 153 was a cut-down L6, so it makes sense that the crank is heavier. I would tend to agree with erikred about extrude-hone, I'd save the money for a header and good exhaust and just port-match by hand. Good luck, and keep us informed!<P>------------------<BR>85 2M4(Lil' Bit)||||reg||1
